Contributing to OceanCloud

Thank you for your interest in contributing to OceanCloud.

OceanCloud welcomes responsible developers and technology professionals from around the world.

This document explains how to apply and how collaboration works.

1. About the project

OceanCloud is an independent technology initiative under active development.

Its long-term plans include cloud infrastructure, networking, domain services, email, defensive cybersecurity, developer tools, social networking and other digital services.

Not all planned services are currently available.

2. Contributor opportunities

We welcome interest in the following areas:

- Backend engineering
- Frontend engineering
- Full-stack development
- API development
- Database engineering
- Cloud infrastructure and DevOps
- Networking
- Defensive cybersecurity
- Software testing and quality assurance
- UI/UX and product design
- Technical writing
- Product planning and documentation

Applicants should identify the area in which they can make a meaningful contribution.

3. Current compensation arrangements

OceanCloud is currently self-funded and cannot offer salaries or guaranteed payments.

Contributions at this stage are voluntary. Applicants must understand and freely accept this arrangement before participating.

Future employment, payment or equity opportunities are not guaranteed. Any future arrangement will require separate discussions and formal written agreements.

4. How to apply

Please introduce yourself through the OceanCloud Community GitHub Discussions or another application channel officially announced by the project.

Include:

1. Your name or professional username.
2. Your country and time zone.
3. Your GitHub profile.
4. Your technical skills.
5. Your previous projects or portfolio.
6. Your preferred OceanCloud division.
7. Your availability and the type of contribution you can realistically make.
8. Why you are interested in OceanCloud.

Never publish passwords, API keys, personal identification documents or private phone numbers in public discussions.

5. Applicant screening

Applications are reviewed individually.

The project may assess:

- Previous technical contributions.
- Practical engineering knowledge.
- Communication and collaboration.
- References where appropriate.
- Understanding of the project's current stage.
- Willingness to follow security and confidentiality rules.

An application does not guarantee acceptance.

6. Trial contributions

Approved applicants may begin with a small, clearly defined evaluation task.

The task, expected result, time commitment and review criteria should be agreed before work begins.

Applicants should not be asked to perform substantial unpaid work as an indefinite trial.

7. Private repository access

OceanCloud's main engineering repository is private.

Access is granted only to approved contributors who need it for their assigned responsibilities.

Contributors must:

- Protect all repository and account credentials.
- Never share private source code without authorization.
- Never publish environment variables, tokens or secrets.
- Avoid making direct changes to the protected main branch.
- Submit proposed changes for review.
- Report security problems privately and responsibly.

Access may be restricted or withdrawn when necessary.

8. Code submission and review

Code contributions must be submitted through GitHub pull requests or another approved process.

Each contribution should explain:

- What was changed.
- Why the change is needed.
- Which files or modules are affected.
- How the change was tested.
- Any security, database or compatibility implications.

Changes may be reviewed, tested, revised or rejected.

A passing automated test does not automatically mean a change is ready for production.

9. Intellectual property and confidentiality

Before making substantial contributions to the private OceanCloud platform, contributors must agree in writing on:

- Ownership and permitted use of contributed code.
- Confidentiality obligations.
- Third-party libraries and their licences.
- Any existing code or intellectual property they bring to the project.
- How contributions may be used if collaboration ends.

No contributor should assume that participation automatically grants company ownership or shares.

Formal agreements must be completed before substantial proprietary contributions are accepted.

10. Professional conduct

All contributors are expected to:

- Communicate respectfully.
- Be honest about their skills and availability.
- Respect other contributors.
- Avoid harassment, discrimination and threats.
- Respect confidentiality.
- Report mistakes and security issues responsibly.

11. Ending collaboration

A contributor may leave voluntarily.

OceanCloud may also end access or collaboration when project requirements, security or conduct make it necessary.

Any outstanding rights and obligations will be handled according to the written agreements.

12. Final note

OceanCloud is being developed step by step.

We value responsible engineering, clear communication, accountability and meaningful contributions.

Thank you for considering joining OceanCloud.

OceanCloud — Building technology, one foundation at a time.
